WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.673
A data pipeline can run on its own, started by a workflow.

00:00:04.673 --> 00:00:08.691
In this video, you create a workflow that runs a pipeline when a

00:00:08.691 --> 00:00:13.340
blog post is published, and a second workflow that reacts when a run fails.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.139
In the list of pipelines, the Actions menu starts a pipeline by hand.

00:00:22.139 --> 00:00:26.395
A workflow can start it when something happens, such as a content item

00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:30.370
being published, or at set times, and it can react to the result.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.804
When the Workflows feature is enabled, the Data Pipelines feature adds two activities,

00:00:36.804 --> 00:00:38.472
in the Data category.

00:00:38.472 --> 00:00:42.920
The Run Data Pipeline Task queues a run, and the Data Pipeline Run

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.380
Completed Event starts a workflow when a run ends.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.747
A run executes the published version of the pipeline, in the background, with

00:00:51.747 --> 00:00:54.305
the access of the user who published it.

00:00:54.305 --> 00:00:58.310
A pipeline that is disabled, or was never published, does not run.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.229
Workflows are under Design, Workflows.

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:10.109
Select Create Workflow, and give the workflow a name that says what it does.

00:01:10.109 --> 00:01:12.430
The other settings have defaults.

00:01:12.889 --> 00:01:16.264
Create and open the designer opens an empty workflow.

00:01:16.264 --> 00:01:20.439
A workflow runs when something happens, so it starts with an event.

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:22.899
Let's choose Content Published Event.

00:01:23.352 --> 00:01:27.155
The Content Types box limits the event to the types you choose.

00:01:27.155 --> 00:01:29.745
Left empty, it runs for any content type.

00:01:29.745 --> 00:01:33.382
The Start badge marks the activity that starts the workflow.

00:01:33.852 --> 00:01:37.160
Select the Done outcome to add the next activity.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:42.152
Search for pipeline, and pick Run Data Pipeline Task, from the Data category.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:53.452
Data pipeline lists the pipelines of the site.

00:01:53.452 --> 00:01:57.573
Choose Blog posts export, which saves the blog posts as a CSV file

00:01:57.573 --> 00:01:59.118
in the media library.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:04.326
The task runs the published version, so publish a pipeline again after you change it.

00:02:04.787 --> 00:02:09.298
Parameters passes values to the run, one name and value per line, for

00:02:09.298 --> 00:02:11.644
the steps of the pipeline to use.

00:02:11.644 --> 00:02:13.087
We leave it empty.

00:02:13.555 --> 00:02:17.472
Wait for the run to end is checked, so the workflow halts until

00:02:17.472 --> 00:02:21.163
the run ends, then follows the Succeeded or Failed outcome.

00:02:21.163 --> 00:02:24.342
Unchecked, the task continues at once with Queued.

00:02:24.342 --> 00:02:27.975
Skip when a run is already queued or running adds Skipped.

00:02:28.443 --> 00:02:32.587
The designer saves a draft as you work, and a draft does not run.

00:02:32.587 --> 00:02:35.623
Publish makes the draft the version that runs.

00:02:39.847 --> 00:02:41.812
Let's publish a blog post.

00:02:41.812 --> 00:02:45.374
In Content Items, open the post, and select Publish.

00:02:45.374 --> 00:02:50.717
The Content Published Event starts the workflow, and the workflow queues the run.

00:02:51.168 --> 00:02:57.044
Under Tools, Data Pipelines, Run History, the run is listed with the Succeeded status.

00:02:57.044 --> 00:02:59.790
Its last badge shows what started it:

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:00.748
Workflow.

00:03:01.218 --> 00:03:04.947
Details lists each step with the rows it read and wrote.

00:03:04.947 --> 00:03:09.638
Under Delivered, the run reports the file it saved to the media library.

00:03:13.938 --> 00:03:19.237
The outcomes of the task cover only the runs that the first workflow starts.

00:03:19.237 --> 00:03:23.954
To react to any run, including one started by hand, create a second

00:03:23.954 --> 00:03:27.638
workflow, in the same way, under Design, Workflows.

00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:33.483
In the Activities pane, search for pipeline, and click Data Pipeline Run Completed.

00:03:33.483 --> 00:03:37.839
This event starts the workflow when a run of a data pipeline ends.

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:42.583
Data pipeline can be one pipeline, or Any data pipeline.

00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:48.659
Runs offers Every run, Runs that succeeded, and Runs that failed or were cancelled.

00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:51.049
Let's choose the failed runs.

00:03:51.543 --> 00:03:55.964
Any activity can follow the event, such as one that sends an email.

00:03:55.964 --> 00:03:57.600
Let's add a Log Task:

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.203
set Log Level to Error, and Text to a sentence that says a run failed.

00:04:04.613 --> 00:04:08.277
Publish the workflow so that it reacts when a run ends.

00:04:08.277 --> 00:04:13.203
Then let's run Blog posts by email, a pipeline that sends the file by email.

00:04:13.678 --> 00:04:17.713
In the list of pipelines, open the Actions menu of Blog posts by

00:04:17.713 --> 00:04:19.097
email, and run it.

00:04:19.097 --> 00:04:22.268
The site has no email settings, so the run fails.

00:04:22.723 --> 00:04:26.943
Under Run History, the run is Failed, and its error says why.

00:04:27.903 --> 00:04:33.633
Under Design, Workflows, Instances, both workflows have an instance, and both are Finished.

00:04:33.633 --> 00:04:36.883
The second one started when the failed run ended.

00:04:38.883 --> 00:04:43.327
The Run Data Pipeline Task starts a run and follows its outcome, and

00:04:43.327 --> 00:04:47.471
the Data Pipeline Run Completed Event reacts when any run ends.

00:04:47.471 --> 00:04:49.033
Thanks for watching.

